> One idea: instead of 20+ levels of mazes plus fixed special levels, like an
> inverted Dungeons of Doom, have 9 or 13 hell levels, drawn from some mix of
> standard demon lord levels (maybe!  they're kind of lame) and these Gehennom
> ideas.  One level is hallucination, one the Courts of Chaos, one land where
> nothing stays dead, or with genocide zombies, etc.  And not fixed levels, just
> randomly generated levels which have the special properties (or meet the
> criteria, like monsters and traps being drawn from specific classes.  And with
> few enough levels and enough of the ideas you'd never know what you'd come
> across until you got there.
> 
> Basically, change Gehennom from long slog of boredom to a briefer run of
> random terror.  Or at least put that Ascension Kit to work.

I like this idea. I agree with the original posters that Hell should
feel different - the rules are different there. Not so radically
different that they make all your preparation useless, but enough to
change your style of play. But any single change applied to all of
Gehennom would probably not be so interesting. However if we game up
with a largish number of possible variants, and randomly selected 9 of
them, it could keep things interesting. It also fits with Dante's
vision that each of the 9 pits had it's own special rules.

For example, if you knew that hell would be full of genocided
monsters, you'd just refrain from using genocide. But if you knew that
_one_ level of hell might _possibly_ be full of genocided monsters,
then the decision is a little trickier. Likewise, one 'level of chaos'
with a randomly changing map would be challenging where a whole bunch
of them would be annoying.

